# Verification — turn 6: conformance/02-external-connector.md

## Overall assessment

This is a complete, well-bounded conformance suite: 60 unique cases (S01–S04, A01–A04, D01–D04, P01–P08, E01–E05, B01–B04, O01–O06, N01–N06, C01–C07, F01–F05, K01–K05) across surface/carrier/authority, describe/identity, prepare/task semantics, start/recovery, bounds, observe/publication, native ingestion, controls/close-time knowledge, artifacts, and shared-evidence/consumer behavior, with hash (§5.1), command-identity-matrix (§6.1), crash-cut (§6.2), cursor-cross-check (§8.1), and authentication/correlation (§9.1) oracles for the hardest paths. No blocking concerns; recommend **Accept with notes**.

Protocol note (recorded once, not blocking): turns 1–6 predate `TURN_END` enforcement, so no `<!-- TURN_END:6 -->` marker exists (`verify.py`: `fence_ok=true`, `marker_ok=false`, `len_ok=true`, `elided_ok=true`). The raw fence (` ```markdown:conformance/02-external-connector.md` … plain ` ``` ` close) is clean, and diffing the raw fence-inner against the tree file shows byte identity apart from a trailing newline — no content lost or duplicated.

## What works

- **Task-shaped boundary held against the governing failure.** §1.1's counterexample table leads with external-tasks-vs-internal-primitives and the Stagehand-on-Juggler failure; `EXT02-S02` rejects `Page`/element/node-handle fields and composite `status`, refusing the shared-browser-abstraction drift the handoff forbids. The nine-verb round-trip (`EXT02-S01`; nine verbs confirmed at contract line 218), command-identity matrix (`EXT02-E02`, `IDEMPOTENCY_CONFLICT` confirmed in contract), and crash-cut oracle (`EXT02-E03`) give identity/recovery real test shape rather than prose.
- **Source-grounded counterexamples.** Spot-checks verify: §13.1's ten M1 requirements reproduce 3-hellscape-blueprint.md §10.6's synthetic-backend list verbatim; the cancellation/continuation, `_elapsed()` zero, MCP-redirect, and `/run`-as-execution-completion claims are all present at the cited blueprint locations (§§1.3/4.5/4.6/5.3/5.5 and finding rows); the carrier assertions (`POST /connector`, UTF-8 JSON, 200 vs 4xx/5xx, unauthenticated-without-correlated-body) match the contract's `x-reference-carrier`; RFC 8785 structured hashing matches `x-hashing`.
- **Epistemic tagging throughout.** 212 tagged claims (17 extracted, 21 inferred, 173 proposed, 1 uncertain). The single `[uncertain]` tag (§1.2, missing original audit artifacts/signing contract) is honest and operationalized in §9.1. The heavy `[proposed]` skew is appropriate for a suite that derives test vectors from a contract rather than quoting sources, and each derivation carries its reasoning tag. Untagged table rows (~all inspected samples) are headers/separators, not substantive claims.
- **Cross-file consistency.** `x-compatibility`, `x-reference-carrier`, `x-contract`/`EXT-TASK`/`EXT-READ-ONLY`, `OperationMapping`, `ConnectorDescriptor`, `WorkScope`, `EXT-EVIDENCE`/`EXT-OBSERVE`/`EXT-NORMALIZE`/`EXT-FAILURE`/`EXT-SURFACE`/`ConnectorLimits`/`SubjectIdentity`, and all 21 `EXT-*` rule IDs in the §13.2 index resolve in contracts/02-external-connector.yaml. Wire versions (`external-connector/0.1.0` plus kernel/lifecycle pins) match the contracts. The `LIFE01-X01`–`LIFE01-X06` cross-references resolve to §7 of conformance/01-application-attempt-lifecycle.md and are a deliberate reuse pin, not drift. `x-native-reconciliation.manual` (contract line 607/646) grounds the §12 manual-receipt scoping. Circus Tent/Ringmaster independence and crawler/manual scoping (§1) match the handoff's product structure.
- **Completeness and scope.** Coherent boundary at §14 (evidence package, verdict/exit discipline reusing the prior suites' vocabulary, contract-review stop condition, ship-independently close). §13 maps both Hellscape M1 requirements and every contract rule to principal cases with an explicit `EXT02-` prefix-carry note. Stays on faithful translation, durable async dispatch, bounded local behavior, and truthful evidence without certifying subject task success.

## Concerns

- **Concerning (not blocking): heaviest `[proposed]`-to-`[extracted]` ratio of the suites so far (173 proposed vs 17 extracted).** More required-result assertions rest on the author's test-design reasoning than on directly cited source lines. That is largely inherent — sources specify mechanics, not test vectors — and the reasoning tags are present rather than missing, with contract-rule citations (§13.2) giving a verifiable anchor. Worth naming so the operator knows this suite leans most on reviewer judgment; turn 13's synthesis is the de facto second check on whether these vectors encode the contract faithfully.
- **Cosmetic: class codes need the legend.** Most case rows use the `H / B / F` code with the §2 legend (F passes on correct refusal/containment/uncertainty — the right pass criterion for adversarial cases) rather than literal class keywords. Defined and consistent; keyword search undercounts adversarial coverage, nothing more.

## Recommendation

**Accept with notes** — proceed; no changes required. No downstream impact beyond normal consumption: this turn has no downstream dependents per `review_deps.json`, and turn 13 builds on its coverage index and counterexample discipline, both intact. Note for turn 13: where this file's case prose and its contract-rule citations could diverge, prefer the contract-rule citations — this file carries the most author-designed (proposed-tagged) content of the conformance suites so far.
